# Extra exam questions, part I: 207 Tier 1 questions so every Tier 1 PI tested on the 11 past DECA Finance exams
# (2018-2026) has practice: 4+ questions for PIs tested on 4+ exams, 2+ for PIs tested on 2-3, 1+ for PIs
# tested once. Topics follow those exams' concepts; questions, scenarios and wording are original.
# Each explanation says in detail why the right answer is correct.
# Format: (PI, stem, [correct answer FIRST, then 3 wrong answers], explanation).
Q = []
def q(pi, stem, opts, exp): Q.append((pi, stem, opts, exp))

# ---------------- Frequently tested (4+ exams) ----------------
q("EC:011","Changes in customers' buying habits, such as a shift to online shopping, create which type of business risk?",
  ["Market (economic) risk","Natural risk","Pure hazard risk","Insurable property risk"],
  "When customer preferences shift, a business's sales can fall even if it does nothing wrong. That's market or economic risk, coming from changes in demand. Natural risks come from weather and disasters, and pure hazard risks like fire can only cause loss.")
q("EI:077","An employee keeps work tasks during work hours and personal errands for after work, so neither interferes with the other. This time-management technique is",
  ["compartmentalizing","procrastinating","multitasking constantly","overcommitting"],
  "Compartmentalizing means setting aside separate blocks of time and attention for different responsibilities, so commitments don't collide. It helps people focus and meet deadlines. Procrastinating and overcommitting make commitments harder to keep.")
q("FI:355","An advisor presents an investment as having a '90% chance of success' instead of a '10% chance of loss' to make it more appealing. This bias is",
  ["framing","incrementalism","diversification","home bias"],
  "Framing is presenting the same information in a way that changes how people react. '90% success' sounds better than '10% failure' though they mean the same thing. Ethical advisors present risks honestly and completely so clients can decide fairly.")
q("NF:110","A good information management system makes sure records can be found quickly when needed. This quality is being",
  ["retrievable","encrypted only","deleted regularly","stored on paper only"],
  "Information has value only if people can find it when they need it. Retrievable records are organized, indexed and searchable, so employees can answer customers, auditors and regulators quickly. Security matters too, but unreachable records are useless.")
q("OP:025","Employees who complete tasks efficiently with fewer errors help control expenses mainly by",
  ["being more productive","ordering more supplies","working unpaid overtime","avoiding company policies"],
  "Productivity means getting more done with the same resources. Efficient, accurate work reduces rework, wasted materials and overtime, which lowers costs. Ordering extra supplies or ignoring policies raises expenses.")
q("EC:014","A manager gives an employee more responsibility and decision-making authority in her current job to make it more meaningful. This is",
  ["job enrichment","division of labor","job obsolescence","task specialization"],
  "Job enrichment adds depth: more responsibility, variety and decision-making, which makes work more rewarding and can counter the boredom of narrow specialization. Division of labor and task specialization narrow jobs instead.")
q("EC:014","Workers who perform one very narrow task may lose their jobs when a machine can do that task. This risk of specialization is",
  ["job obsolescence","job enrichment","cross-training","promotion"],
  "Job obsolescence happens when a job is no longer needed because technology or process changes replace it. Very specialized tasks are easiest to automate, which is one downside of narrow specialization. Cross-training reduces that risk.")
q("FI:002","A credit card that lets you borrow up to a limit, repay and borrow again is which type of credit?",
  ["Revolving credit","Installment credit","Service credit","A secured car loan"],
  "Revolving credit, like credit cards and home equity lines, gives a credit limit you can use, repay and reuse over and over. Installment credit is a fixed amount repaid in set payments, like a car loan or mortgage.")
q("FI:002","When comparing credit cards, which cost is charged every year just for having the card, whether or not you carry a balance?",
  ["An annual fee","The APR","A late fee","A balance transfer fee"],
  "An annual fee is charged yearly simply for having the card, regardless of use. The APR applies only to balances you carry, late fees only if you pay late, and balance transfer fees only when you move debt.")
q("FI:579","For financial information to be useful in decisions, it must be",
  ["reliable and consistent","exaggerated","kept secret from managers","changed each month to look better"],
  "Managers, lenders and investors can only make good decisions with information that's accurate (reliable) and prepared the same way over time (consistent), so periods can be compared. Exaggerated or manipulated numbers lead to bad decisions.")
q("FI:579","A café owner's financial records show that coffee costs have risen 25% while her prices haven't changed. Using this information, she will most likely",
  ["raise her prices or find cheaper suppliers","stop keeping records","ignore the change","give away free coffee"],
  "Financial information reveals problems like rising costs squeezing profit. Seeing that costs rose while prices stayed flat tells the owner to act by adjusting prices, renegotiating with suppliers or cutting waste. That's exactly why businesses need financial information.")
q("MK:015","Which employee action best helps a bank reach its goal of cutting operating costs by 5%?",
  ["Turning off unused equipment and reducing paper use","Leaving lights on overnight","Printing every email","Ordering supplies in excess"],
  "Employees help achieve company goals through everyday actions. Turning off unused equipment and cutting paper use directly lower costs. The other actions waste money and work against the goal.")
q("MK:015","A theme park employee learns where every attraction is so she can answer visitors' questions courteously. Her action helps the park",
  ["achieve its customer-satisfaction goals","reduce its marketing budget","avoid serving guests","raise ticket prices"],
  "Employees contribute to company results when they prepare to serve customers well. Knowing the park lets her give quick, friendly answers, which improves guests' experience and supports the park's goals for satisfaction and repeat visits.")
q("OP:001","After defining a project's purpose and scope, a project team estimates the cost of every task. It is",
  ["translating the plan into a realistic budget","closing the project","celebrating results","writing the final report"],
  "A project plan must include a realistic budget, so the team estimates costs for each task and totals them. That shows whether the project is affordable and gives a standard to track spending against.")
q("OP:001","When developing a project plan, the team lists everything that could go wrong, such as delays or supplier problems. This step is",
  ["identifying as many known risks as possible","ignoring risk","approving final payment","measuring final success"],
  "Good project plans identify known risks early and plan responses, such as backup suppliers or extra time. That keeps surprises from derailing the schedule and budget.")
q("OP:015","A manufacturer decides whether to make a part itself or buy it from a supplier by comparing its production capabilities with the cost of making the part. This is a decision in",
  ["purchasing (make-or-buy)","advertising","human resources","customer service"],
  "Purchasing includes make-or-buy decisions: comparing the cost and capacity to produce an item internally with the cost of buying it. The choice depends on cost, quality, capacity and control.")
q("OP:015","A store buys products from wholesalers to sell to customers at a profit. This type of purchasing is",
  ["purchasing for resale","industrial purchasing for production","government purchasing","personal purchasing"],
  "Retailers and wholesalers buy goods for resale, buying products to sell to others at a markup. Industrial purchasing buys materials and equipment used to make products or run a business.")
q("PD:012","Why should a manager recognize that creativity shows up in different ways?",
  ["People show creativity in a variety of ways, such as solving problems or improving processes","Only artists are creative","Creativity can't be used in finance","Creativity always means drawing"],
  "Creativity isn't limited to art. In finance it shows up as a new way to solve a client problem, a better process or a clearer presentation. Recognizing different forms of creativity helps managers use everyone's strengths.")
q("PD:012","An analyst adjusts her approach when a client doesn't understand her first explanation and tries a new example. She is showing",
  ["flexibility, an important part of creativity","stubbornness","passive behavior","procrastination"],
  "Flexibility, willingness to change your approach and try new ideas, is a key part of creativity. Finding another way to explain something until it clicks is creative problem-solving in action.")
q("PD:018","Writing down personal goals and reviewing them regularly mainly helps you",
  ["clarify your goals and stay focused on them","avoid all hard work","guarantee instant success","eliminate the need to plan"],
  "Writing goals down clarifies exactly what you want and makes them easier to remember and track. Regular review keeps you focused and lets you adjust. It supports hard work and planning rather than replacing them.")
q("PD:018","Setting clear personal goals contributes most directly to",
  ["achieving success in life by focusing effort","avoiding responsibility","making decisions randomly","reducing motivation"],
  "Clear goals focus time and energy on what matters most, which makes success more likely, whether in school, career or finances. They give direction and motivation, the opposite of random decisions.")
q("CO:087","To show the key dates in a client's financial plan, such as the emergency fund by June and a 529 opened in September, the best graphic aid is a",
  ["timeline","pie chart","scatter plot","organizational chart"],
  "A timeline shows events in order along a line of time, so it's ideal for a plan's milestones and deadlines. Pie charts show parts of a whole, scatter plots show relationships between two variables and org charts show reporting structure.")
q("CO:087","A loan officer wants to show customers the steps in the mortgage approval process, from application to closing. The best graphic aid is a",
  ["flowchart","pie chart","line graph","bar chart of interest rates"],
  "A flowchart shows the steps of a process and the order they happen in, with arrows connecting each step. It makes a multi-step process like mortgage approval easy to follow.")
q("EC:045","When meeting a business client from another country, a finance professional should avoid unfamiliar hand gestures mainly because",
  ["gestures can mean different, even offensive, things in other cultures","gestures are illegal in business","all gestures mean the same everywhere","gestures always speed up deals"],
  "Nonverbal signals vary by culture. A gesture that's friendly in one country can be rude in another. Avoiding unfamiliar gestures and learning local customs prevents accidental offense and builds trust.")
q("EC:045","A company entering a new foreign market studies local customs, values and communication styles first. Its main goal is to",
  ["avoid being insulting or rude and build good relationships","copy its home-country approach exactly","avoid communicating with local customers","reduce product quality"],
  "Cultural and social environments affect how business is done. Learning local customs helps a company communicate respectfully and avoid mistakes that could insult customers or partners and hurt sales.")
q("EC:072","Taxes charged on specific products such as gasoline and cigarettes are called",
  ["excise taxes","income taxes","property taxes","payroll taxes"],
  "Excise taxes apply to specific goods, often gasoline, tobacco and alcohol, and are usually included in the price. Income taxes apply to earnings, property taxes to real estate and payroll taxes to wages.")
q("EC:072","The largest source of revenue for the U.S. federal government is the",
  ["individual income tax","excise tax","estate tax","customs duty"],
  "The individual income tax brings in about half of all federal revenue, more than any other source. Payroll taxes for Social Security and Medicare are second. Excise taxes, estate taxes and customs duties are much smaller shares.")
q("EI:008","In a team meeting, Jordan calmly says, 'I see it differently, and here's why,' then listens to others. Jordan is being",
  ["assertive","aggressive","passive","manipulative"],
  "Assertive communication expresses your own views honestly and confidently while respecting others. Jordan disagrees calmly and still listens, the definition of assertiveness. Aggressive behavior disregards others; passive behavior hides your view.")
q("EI:008","Which behavior shows assertiveness when meeting new colleagues?",
  ["Starting conversations and introducing yourself","Waiting for others to speak to you first","Interrupting to talk about yourself","Avoiding eye contact"],
  "Initiating conversations, introducing yourself and engaging others shows confident, assertive behavior. Waiting silently is passive, and interrupting to talk about yourself is aggressive.")
q("EN:039","The first step in the entrepreneurial process is usually",
  ["idea generation: identifying a need or want to fill","hiring employees","applying for a business license","opening a second location"],
  "Entrepreneurship starts with idea generation, spotting a need or want that isn't being met and an idea for meeting it. Licensing, hiring and expansion come later, after the idea is tested and the business is planned.")
q("EN:039","Successful entrepreneurs build businesses mainly by",
  ["creating products or services that meet a need or want","avoiding all risk","copying competitors exactly","working only as employees"],
  "Businesses succeed when they meet customers' needs or wants better than alternatives. Entrepreneurs take risks to create those solutions. Avoiding all risk or simply copying competitors rarely builds a lasting business.")
q("EN:044","A start-up founder regularly makes employees work 70-hour weeks without extra pay. Which ethical problem is most likely to hurt the business?",
  ["Overburdened employees are unlikely to stay with the company","Employees will become too productive","Customers will pay higher prices","The business will pay fewer taxes"],
  "Treating employees unfairly by overworking and underpaying them is unethical and drives good people away. High turnover raises hiring and training costs and hurts service. Ethical treatment of employees is part of business ethics in entrepreneurship.")
q("EN:044","Why do customers increasingly expect small businesses to act ethically?",
  ["Customers want to support companies that share their values and treat people fairly","Ethics lowers prices to zero","Laws require customers to check ethics","Customers never compare businesses"],
  "Many customers consider a company's ethics, such as honesty, fair treatment of workers and environmental impact, when deciding where to buy. For small businesses that depend on reputation, ethical behavior builds loyalty and word of mouth.")
q("FI:093","A bank reviews a company's balance sheet before approving a loan mainly to see",
  ["its current financial situation: what it owns and owes","its advertising plan","its employees' schedules","its customers' names"],
  "The balance sheet shows the company's assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time, its current financial situation. Lenders use it to judge whether the company can take on more debt and whether it has assets to support the loan.")
q("FI:351","An accountant refuses a gift from a client whose books she is auditing. She is",
  ["avoiding a potential conflict of interest","being unfriendly","violating accounting rules","practicing front-running"],
  "Accepting gifts from a client you audit could influence, or appear to influence, your judgment. Refusing protects her independence and objectivity, core ethical standards for accountants.")
q("FI:351","Why must auditors behave independently from the companies they audit?",
  ["So their opinions on the financial statements are objective and trustworthy","So they can charge higher fees","So they can skip testing","So companies can choose the results"],
  "Investors rely on auditors to give an unbiased opinion on whether financial statements are fair. If auditors depend on or favor the client, their opinions lose value. That's why rules limit auditors' financial ties to their clients.")
q("NF:139","When analyzing data, an analyst should make sure the results are",
  ["unbiased, letting the data speak rather than favoring a desired answer","adjusted to match what her boss wants","based only on data that supports her idea","rounded to whole thousands always"],
  "Good data analysis is unbiased: the analyst follows the evidence even if it doesn't support what she expected. Cherry-picking data or shaping results to please someone leads to wrong decisions.")
q("NF:139","A bank studies five years of transaction data to estimate how many customers will apply for mortgages next spring. This type of analysis is",
  ["predictive","descriptive only","prescriptive budgeting","data entry"],
  "Predictive analysis uses historical data to forecast future outcomes, here mortgage demand. Descriptive analysis only summarizes what already happened. Banks use predictions to plan staffing and lending capacity.")
q("PD:036","Joining a local chamber of commerce helps a finance professional's development mainly by",
  ["providing networking, events and local business knowledge","replacing all licensing exams","setting interest rates","guaranteeing a promotion"],
  "Chambers of commerce connect local business people through events, programs and networking, which builds relationships and market knowledge. They don't replace licenses or guarantee promotions.")
q("PD:036","Many businesses join trade associations partly because the associations help members",
  ["promote their industry and products","avoid all competition","set illegal fixed prices","skip regulations"],
  "Trade associations promote their industry, share research and best practices, offer training and advocate on laws affecting members. They can't legally fix prices, and members still compete and follow regulations.")
q("SM:001","Arranging people, tasks and resources so a plan can be carried out is which management function?",
  ["Organizing","Planning","Controlling","Forecasting"],
  "Organizing comes after planning: deciding who does what, setting up departments, assigning responsibilities and gathering resources. Planning sets goals, and controlling measures results against them.")
q("SM:001","A branch manager checks daily reports, compares them with goals and coaches employees who fall behind. She is mainly performing which management functions?",
  ["Controlling and directing","Planning and staffing","Organizing and forecasting","Recruiting and advertising"],
  "Comparing results with goals is controlling, and guiding and coaching employees is directing (leading). Managers move among planning, organizing, directing and controlling every day.")

# ---------------- Tested on 2-3 exams ----------------
q("BL:126","Customs regulations and tariffs on imported goods are often meant to",
  ["protect a country's economy from foreign competition","eliminate all trade","lower prices on imports","replace income taxes"],
  "Customs regulations control what enters a country, and tariffs (customs duties) make imports more expensive, which helps protect domestic businesses from foreign competition. They also collect revenue and enforce safety and security rules.")
q("BL:126","A tax a government collects on goods brought into the country is a",
  ["customs duty (tariff)","sales tax","property tax","payroll tax"],
  "A customs duty, or tariff, is a tax on imported goods, collected at the border by customs officials (U.S. Customs and Border Protection). Importers pay it, and the cost is often passed on to buyers.")
q("BL:163","A bank follows not just the exact wording of a consumer-protection law but also its purpose of treating customers fairly. The bank is complying with",
  ["the spirit as well as the letter of the law","only the letter of the law","no law at all","a loophole"],
  "The letter of the law is its exact wording; the spirit is its intent. Ethical businesses follow both, avoiding technically legal tricks that defeat a law's purpose. Exploiting loopholes may be legal but damages trust.")
q("BL:163","Employees who take part in a lawful public protest should",
  ["follow laws and avoid violence and vandalism","damage property to make a point","block emergency vehicles","ignore police instructions"],
  "Peaceful protest is legal, but violence, vandalism and blocking emergency services are not. Complying with the spirit and intent of laws means expressing views lawfully and respecting others' rights and safety.")
q("CO:016","Effective business writing should be clear and concise so that readers can",
  ["easily understand it","admire long sentences","guess the meaning","skip it"],
  "The goal of business writing is understanding. Clear words, short sentences and logical organization let readers grasp the message quickly and act on it. Long, complicated writing wastes time and causes errors.")
q("CO:025","In a presentation, a speaker's posture, gestures, facial expressions and eye contact are all part of",
  ["nonverbal communication","the thesis","the bibliography","written communication"],
  "Nonverbal communication is everything you communicate without words: posture, gestures, facial expressions and eye contact. It affects how confident and credible a presenter seems, as in a DECA roleplay.")
q("CO:031","An effective persuasive message to clients should",
  ["describe the benefits to the audience","focus only on the company's needs","avoid any call to action","use as much jargon as possible"],
  "Persuasion works when the audience sees what's in it for them. Explaining benefits, like saving money, reducing risk or reaching goals, motivates action. Persuasive messages usually end with a clear call to action.")
q("CO:055","A student highlights key points, asks questions and summarizes as she reads a long financial report. She is using",
  ["active reading","skimming only","passive reading","speed reading without comprehension"],
  "Active reading means engaging with the text by highlighting, questioning and summarizing, which improves understanding and helps pull out the most relevant information.")
q("CO:055","A report says Company A had 27% market share, Company B 24% and Company C 19%. What is the most relevant fact for an investor comparing market leaders?",
  ["Company A leads, with B close behind","Company C leads the market","All three have equal share","Market share is never relevant"],
  "Extracting relevant information means identifying the facts that answer your question. Here, Company A has the largest share (27%) and B is close (24%), the key comparison for judging market leadership.")
q("CO:084","An advisor presenting a retirement plan to a company's board of directors should use a",
  ["formal communication style","casual style with slang","style full of inside jokes","style that avoids eye contact"],
  "Adapt your style to your audience. A board of directors expects formal, polished, well-organized communication. With a young first-time client, a warmer, simpler style that builds rapport may work better.")
q("CO:090","A professional email signature should include your",
  ["name, position, company and contact information","favorite quote and emojis","home address and Social Security number","password hint"],
  "A professional signature tells recipients who you are and how to reach you: name, title, company and phone or email. Never include sensitive personal information like a Social Security number.")
q("CO:092","An employee needs to discuss a sensitive performance issue with a team member. The best communication channel is",
  ["a private face-to-face conversation","a group email","a text message","a public social media post"],
  "Sensitive or personal topics are best handled face to face, privately, where tone and body language help communication and the person can respond. Group emails, texts and public posts can embarrass people and cause misunderstandings.")
q("CO:094","A short internal document that tells employees about a new procedure is usually written as a",
  ["memorandum (memo)","press release","sales letter","résumé"],
  "A memo is a brief internal written message used to share information like new procedures, policies or meeting notices within an organization. Press releases and sales letters go to outside audiences.")
q("CO:094","A report that updates managers on how far a project has advanced and what remains to be done is a",
  ["progress report","financial statement","job application","marketing brochure"],
  "A progress report explains what's been completed, what's still left, any problems and whether the project is on schedule and budget. It keeps managers informed so they can make decisions.")
q("CO:133","In a business letter, the inside address and salutation appear",
  ["before the body of the letter","after the signature","only on the envelope","in the middle of the body"],
  "Standard business letter format places the date, inside address (recipient's name and address) and salutation ('Dear Ms. Lee:') before the body, then the closing and signature after it.")
q("CO:133","A good business letter gets to the point without unnecessary words. This quality is",
  ["conciseness","wordiness","vagueness","informality"],
  "Conciseness means saying what you need in as few words as possible without losing clarity. Busy readers appreciate letters that get to the point. Wordy or vague letters waste time and may be misunderstood.")
q("CO:204","Turning a dense table of investment returns into an infographic is likely to make readers",
  ["pay more attention and understand the data faster","ignore the data","distrust all charts","read more slowly"],
  "Data visualization, like infographics, charts and heat maps, makes information easier to grasp and more engaging, so audiences pay more attention and remember it. That's why charts help in roleplays.")
q("CR:003","Which action best builds positive customer relations when a client explains a problem?",
  ["Listen respectfully without interrupting","Finish their sentences to save time","Look at your phone","Argue about the details"],
  "Positive customer relations start with respectful listening. Letting customers explain fully shows you value them and helps you understand the real problem. Interrupting, being distracted or arguing damages the relationship.")
q("CR:003","Positive customer relations benefit a business partly by",
  ["avoiding wasted time and resources spent on complaints and lost customers","guaranteeing no complaints ever","removing the need for products","raising prices automatically"],
  "Good customer relations prevent many problems, so fewer complaints, refunds and lost customers save time and money. Keeping existing customers is also far cheaper than finding new ones.")
q("CR:007","A bank teller explains to a customer why a deposit hold policy exists. Clear explanations of business policies help customers",
  ["understand and accept the policies","ignore the policies","avoid ever banking again","get policies changed instantly"],
  "When employees explain the reason behind a policy, such as holds protecting against bad checks, customers are more likely to understand and accept it. Unexplained rules feel arbitrary and frustrate customers.")
q("CR:009","A customer seems suspicious and questions every fee. The employee should",
  ["stay patient, explain clearly and provide documentation","become defensive","refuse to answer","tell the customer to leave"],
  "Difficult customers, including suspicious ones, can be any age or background. Patience, clear explanations and written documentation build trust and resolve concerns. Getting defensive or refusing to answer makes them more suspicious.")
q("CR:016","Strong customer relationship management usually leads to greater",
  ["customer loyalty","customer turnover","marketing costs per customer","complaints"],
  "CRM focuses on understanding and serving customers well over time, which builds loyalty, so customers stay longer and buy more. Loyal customers cost less to keep than new ones cost to win.")
q("CR:016","Satisfied, loyal customers often recommend a business to friends and family. This benefit of CRM is",
  ["word-of-mouth promotion","price fixing","inventory shrinkage","a tax deduction"],
  "Happy customers become advocates, sharing positive experiences with others. That word-of-mouth promotion is free, trusted marketing, and it's one of the biggest benefits of strong customer relationships.")
q("CR:019","When meeting clients from other cultures, a simple way to show respect is to",
  ["learn to pronounce their names correctly","give them nicknames","ignore cultural differences","speak louder"],
  "Pronouncing someone's name correctly shows respect and care, building trust from the start. Asking politely how to say a name is better than guessing or using a nickname they didn't choose.")
q("EC:001","The economic activity of creating goods and services is called",
  ["production","consumption","distribution","marketing"],
  "Production is creating goods and services. Consumption is using them, and distribution is moving them to customers. All three are basic economic activities.")
q("EC:003","Which is an example of a capital resource used to produce goods and services?",
  ["A highway used to transport goods","A natural lake","A worker's skills","An entrepreneur's idea"],
  "Capital resources are human-made tools and structures used in production: highways, buildings, machinery and equipment. A lake is a natural (land) resource, skills are labor and ideas are entrepreneurship.")
q("EC:005","A hurricane destroys much of the region's orange crop. According to supply and demand, this natural disaster will most likely",
  ["decrease supply and raise prices","increase supply and lower prices","have no effect","decrease demand to zero"],
  "Natural disasters can sharply cut supply. With fewer oranges available and demand about the same, prices rise. Supply and demand explain how events like disasters affect prices.")
q("EC:008","Laws that set minimum safety standards in factories are an example of government",
  ["regulating workplace conditions","owning private businesses","setting all prices","eliminating competition"],
  "The government regulates business to protect workers, consumers and the public. Workplace safety laws, enforced by OSHA, are a common example. In a market economy, the government regulates but doesn't own most businesses or set prices.")
q("EC:008","When the government spends more than it collects in taxes, it must",
  ["increase borrowing by issuing Treasury securities","stop all spending immediately","print money for every bill","eliminate taxes"],
  "A budget deficit means spending exceeds revenue, so the federal government borrows by selling Treasury bills, notes and bonds. That borrowing adds to the national debt and can affect interest rates for businesses.")
q("EC:010","A business's main reason for operating is to",
  ["make a profit by meeting customer needs","avoid all customers","spend as much as possible","pay the highest taxes"],
  "For-profit businesses exist to earn a profit by providing goods and services customers want. Profit rewards owners for taking risk and funds growth. Factors like costs, prices and competition all affect it.")
q("EC:012","Hotels in a city offer special weekend room rates to attract travelers away from rivals. This is an example of",
  ["competition","a monopoly","price fixing","government regulation"],
  "Competition is rivalry among businesses for customers, often through prices, quality and service. Special rates to win travelers from other hotels show competition benefiting consumers. Price fixing would be an illegal agreement among rivals.")
q("EC:013","A bank measures how many loan applications each employee processes per day. It is measuring",
  ["productivity","inflation","market share","GDP"],
  "Productivity is output per unit of input, such as loan applications processed per employee per day. Higher productivity lowers costs per unit and raises profit. Inflation, market share and GDP measure other things.")
q("EC:013","A company improves training, equipment and the work environment so employees can do more in less time. This improves both productivity and employees'",
  ["quality of work life","tax bracket","credit score","insurance deductible"],
  "Better tools, training and working conditions improve quality of work life, which also tends to raise productivity because satisfied, well-equipped employees work more effectively.")
q("EC:017","Reducing government purchases, with everything else unchanged, would directly cause GDP to",
  ["decrease","increase","stay exactly the same","double"],
  "GDP = consumer spending + business investment + government purchases + net exports. Cutting government purchases lowers one component, so GDP decreases unless other spending rises to offset it.")
q("EC:023","A factory adds more and more workers to the same machines, and eventually each new worker adds less output than the one before. This illustrates",
  ["the law of diminishing returns","economies of scale","the law of demand","inflation"],
  "The law of diminishing returns says that adding more of one input to a fixed amount of another eventually yields smaller increases in output, as workers get in each other's way. It explains why costs per unit can start rising.")
q("EC:023","If adding one more worker actually reduces total output because of crowding, the worker's marginal return is",
  ["negative","positive and rising","zero by law","unlimited"],
  "When an added input lowers total output, its marginal return is negative. That's the extreme end of diminishing returns, a sign the business has more of that input than its fixed resources can use.")
q("EC:071","Promoting, pricing and distributing products to customers are which type of business activity?",
  ["Marketing","Production","Accounting","Management"],
  "Marketing activities include product planning, pricing, promotion, distribution and selling, everything involved in getting products to customers. Production creates them, and finance and accounting manage money.")
q("EC:071","Evaluating a company's ratios to decide whether it can afford to expand is part of which business activity?",
  ["Finance (financial analysis)","Marketing","Production","Human resources"],
  "Finance manages money: obtaining funds, investing them and analyzing financial performance. Financial analysis of whether the company can afford expansion is a finance activity.")
q("EC:106","A company that cuts corners on safety to save money most directly increases its",
  ["financial risk from lawsuits, fines and lost sales","customer loyalty","employee morale","credit rating"],
  "Unethical choices create financial risk: lawsuits, regulatory fines, recalls and lost customers can cost far more than the money saved. Business ethics and financial health are closely linked.")
q("EC:106","When no specific rule covers a situation, an employee's decision about what's right is guided mainly by",
  ["his personal code of ethics","the stock price","the weather","his commute time"],
  "A personal code of ethics, an individual's values and principles about right and wrong, guides behavior when laws or policies don't give a clear answer. Companies also provide codes of conduct to support ethical decisions.")
q("EI:006","A bank's procedures change overnight because of a new regulation. An adaptable employee will",
  ["adjust to the new situation and learn the new steps","refuse to follow the new procedures","complain until the rules change back","quit immediately"],
  "Adaptability means adjusting quickly and positively when circumstances change. In finance, rules, technology and markets change often, so adaptable employees are highly valued.")
q("EI:006","Volunteering to learn a new software system that intimidates you shows you are",
  ["willing to leave your comfort zone","resistant to change","avoiding growth","being passive"],
  "Adaptable people are willing to step outside their comfort zone to learn and grow. Taking on something unfamiliar builds skills and confidence and shows employers you can handle change.")
q("EI:015","Two departments disagree over a shared budget, and their managers meet to work out a compromise that gives each part of what it wants. They are",
  ["negotiating","avoiding","forcing","ignoring the conflict"],
  "Negotiating is a conflict-resolution skill in which parties discuss their interests and reach an agreement both can accept, often through compromise. Avoiding ignores the conflict, and forcing imposes one side's will.")
q("EI:016","People with healthy self-esteem generally see themselves as",
  ["valuable and capable","worthless","better than everyone else","unable to grow"],
  "Self-esteem is how you value yourself. Healthy self-esteem means seeing yourself as worthy and capable while staying realistic, not arrogant. It supports confidence, resilience and good relationships.")
q("EI:016","Which experience most often builds self-esteem?",
  ["Achieving a goal you worked hard for","Being criticized constantly","Avoiding all challenges","Comparing yourself to everyone online"],
  "Achievement, setting a goal, working for it and succeeding, is a powerful builder of self-esteem because it proves your abilities to yourself. Constant criticism and comparison tend to lower self-esteem.")
q("EI:021","A responsible employee balances her own needs with those of others by",
  ["completing her work reliably while helping teammates when they need it","doing only what benefits herself","ignoring deadlines","taking credit for others' work"],
  "Responsible behavior means being dependable and accountable while considering others: doing your share, meeting commitments and supporting the team. Self-centered behavior, missed deadlines and taking credit undermine trust.")
q("EI:021","An employee admits she forgot to file a report and stays late to finish it. She is demonstrating",
  ["responsibility","aggressiveness","passivity","procrastination"],
  "Responsibility means owning your duties and mistakes and fixing them. Admitting the error and finishing the work shows accountability, which builds trust with managers and teammates.")
q("EI:023","Which habit can help someone feel more self-confident before an important presentation?",
  ["Preparing thoroughly and dressing in professional clothes that feel good","Skipping practice","Expecting to fail","Avoiding eye contact"],
  "Confidence grows from preparation and from small things that help you feel ready, like professional clothing that fits well. Practicing builds competence, and competence builds confidence.")
q("EI:024","An employee who finishes her assignments and asks her manager for additional work is showing",
  ["initiative","laziness","passive behavior","conflict avoidance"],
  "Initiative means taking action without being told, such as seeking more responsibility, offering ideas or solving problems proactively. Employees with initiative stand out and are often considered for promotions.")
q("EI:026","Employees who adjust well to change at work often",
  ["make positive changes of their own","resist every new idea","wait for change to reverse","blame others for change"],
  "People who handle change well see it as unavoidable and look for opportunities in it, often suggesting improvements themselves. Resisting or waiting for things to go back makes adjusting harder.")
q("EI:027","A person who sets high standards, takes calculated risks and persists toward goals shows",
  ["an achievement orientation","a fear of failure","passivity","indifference"],
  "Achievement orientation is a drive to set challenging goals, work toward excellence and persist through obstacles. It's often linked with an entrepreneurial spirit and leadership.")
q("EI:027","An employee with strong achievement orientation is most likely to",
  ["seek challenging goals and lead efforts to meet them","avoid responsibility","settle for minimum effort","wait to be told what to do"],
  "People driven to achieve take on challenging goals, often stepping into leadership roles to accomplish them. They measure themselves by results and keep improving, unlike those who do the minimum.")
q("EI:030","A financial advisor says, 'I can see this decision is stressful for you,' before discussing options. She is showing",
  ["empathy","sympathy without understanding","aggressiveness","indifference"],
  "Empathy means understanding and acknowledging another person's feelings. Recognizing a client's stress before moving to solutions makes them feel heard and builds trust.")
q("EI:037","A manager who makes every decision alone and expects employees to obey without input has which leadership style?",
  ["Authoritarian (autocratic)","Democratic","Laissez-faire","Collaborative"],
  "Authoritarian (autocratic) leaders make decisions alone and expect compliance. It can work in emergencies but often hurts morale and working relationships. Democratic leaders involve the team; laissez-faire leaders give it free rein.")
q("EI:037","The foundation of positive working relationships is mutual",
  ["respect","competition","suspicion","avoidance"],
  "Positive working relationships rest on mutual respect: valuing others' ideas, time and contributions. Respect leads to trust, better communication and more effective teamwork.")
q("EI:045","Employees who work on effective teams often report higher",
  ["job satisfaction","stress from isolation","turnover","conflict"],
  "Being part of a supportive, effective team gives people a sense of belonging, shared success and help when needed, which raises job satisfaction and engagement.")
q("EI:045","A small group of employees meets regularly to find and solve work-quality problems. This is a",
  ["quality circle","chain of command","board of directors","focus group of customers"],
  "A quality circle is a small team of employees who meet regularly to identify, analyze and solve quality problems in their work area. It uses front-line knowledge and builds teamwork.")
q("EI:059","An employee is bored with routine tasks. A manager motivates him by",
  ["giving him a new, challenging project","ignoring the problem","reducing his responsibilities further","criticizing him publicly"],
  "Many people are motivated by challenge, growth and meaningful work. A new, challenging project can renew engagement. Ignoring boredom or reducing responsibilities usually lowers motivation further.")
q("EI:059","According to motivation theory, once a need such as fair pay is satisfied, it",
  ["no longer motivates much, so other factors like recognition and growth matter more","motivates employees forever","becomes the only thing employees care about","has no effect on morale"],
  "Satisfied needs stop driving behavior strongly (as in Maslow's hierarchy and Herzberg's theory). Once pay feels fair, people are motivated more by recognition, achievement, responsibility and growth.")
q("EI:060","An effective shared vision for a team should be",
  ["clear enough to be summed up concisely","long and complicated","kept secret from the team","changed every week"],
  "A good vision is clear, memorable and concise, so everyone understands and can repeat it. That clarity aligns people's efforts. A vision no one can remember or understand can't guide a team.")
q("EI:060","Why does a shared vision help a team?",
  ["It focuses team members' energy on common goals","It removes the need for communication","It guarantees no conflict","It lets members ignore deadlines"],
  "A shared vision gives everyone a common direction, so effort goes toward the same goals instead of competing priorities. It also motivates people by connecting their work to something meaningful.")
q("EI:075","After a client complains, an advisor says, 'That was my decision, and I'll fix it.' He is",
  ["accepting responsibility for his decision","blaming others","avoiding the issue","being passive"],
  "Taking responsibility means owning your decisions and their results, especially when things go wrong. That builds trust and credibility. Blaming others damages relationships and reputation.")
q("EI:075","Which behavior shows a failure to take responsibility?",
  ["Blaming a coworker for your own mistake","Admitting an error and correcting it","Asking for help when needed","Following through on promises"],
  "Blaming others for your own mistakes avoids accountability and damages trust. Responsible people admit errors, fix them, ask for help when needed and keep their promises.")
q("EI:092","A new analyst is given an assignment with incomplete information and stays calm while working through it. She is showing",
  ["tolerance for ambiguity","herd mentality","procrastination","aggressiveness"],
  "Tolerance for ambiguity is the ability to stay calm and productive when information is unclear or incomplete. In finance, decisions often must be made without perfect information.")
q("EI:124","Having strong moral principles and sticking to them even when it's difficult is called",
  ["integrity","an ethical dilemma","herd mentality","office politics"],
  "Integrity means being honest and consistent with your values even under pressure. An ethical dilemma is a situation where values conflict, and integrity helps you choose the right action.")
q("EI:127","A manager who applies the same attendance rules to every employee, including friends, is being",
  ["fair and consistent","biased","inconsistent","lenient only with some"],
  "Fairness means treating people consistently, using the same standards for everyone regardless of personal relationships. Inconsistent treatment damages trust and morale and can create legal risk.")
q("EI:127","People feel they are being treated fairly when they believe they",
  ["are being treated the way they deserve to be treated","always get everything they want","never receive feedback","are treated differently from everyone"],
  "Perceived fairness comes from believing that treatment matches what you've earned or deserve, with consistent rules and honest processes. Fairness isn't getting everything you want; it's being treated justly.")
q("EI:128","A coworker always delivers her part of a project on time and as promised. Her teammates trust her because of her",
  ["dependability","popularity","job title","salary"],
  "Trust grows from dependability: consistently doing what you said you'd do. Popularity or position doesn't build lasting trust; reliable behavior over time does.")
q("EI:132","When leaders consistently act ethically, employees are more likely to",
  ["support the organization's mission and behave ethically too","break rules more often","distrust leadership","ignore company values"],
  "Leaders set the tone. When they model ethical behavior, employees see that values matter, trust leadership more and are more motivated to support the mission and act ethically themselves.")
q("EI:132","Holding yourself to the same rules you expect of others and accepting consequences for your actions demonstrates",
  ["accountability","favoritism","entitlement","avoidance"],
  "Accountability means taking ownership of your actions and their results and holding yourself to the same standards as others. It's essential to modeling ethical behavior.")
q("EI:134","A team agrees with a flawed plan because no one wants to disagree, and the plan fails. This is an example of",
  ["groupthink","consensus building","healthy debate","due diligence"],
  "Groupthink happens when the desire for harmony leads a group to accept ideas without critical evaluation. Challenging the status quo and inviting dissent helps teams avoid it.")
q("EI:136","Taking time to understand viewpoints that conflict with your own can lead to",
  ["improved communication and better decisions","more misunderstandings","weaker relationships","less information"],
  "Considering opposing views exposes you to new information and helps you communicate more effectively. Decisions improve because more perspectives are weighed.")
q("EI:136","Listening carefully to a colleague's opposing argument helps you become more",
  ["empathetic","closed-minded","aggressive","biased"],
  "Understanding why someone disagrees helps you see things from their perspective, which builds empathy. Empathy improves relationships and makes it easier to find solutions everyone can accept.")
q("EI:137","A manager treats two employees differently in the same situation because she likes one more. This mainly shows a failure to consider",
  ["fairness and the long-term impact of actions on others","the stock price","advertising results","interest rates"],
  "Treating people differently in the same situation is unfair and can damage trust, morale and relationships over time. Considering the long-term impact of actions on others means applying consistent standards.")
q("EI:137","An employee decides before acting without understanding all the facts, causing problems for others. This gap in understanding is a",
  ["knowledge gap","conflict of interest","sunk cost","credit risk"],
  "A knowledge gap is missing information needed for a good decision. Acting without closing it can harm others. Assessing the long-term impact of actions means getting the facts first.")
q("FI:059","Bitcoin and Ethereum are examples of",
  ["cryptocurrency","government bonds","Federal Reserve notes","Treasury notes"],
  "Cryptocurrencies are digital currencies that run on decentralized blockchain networks and aren't issued by governments. Federal Reserve notes are U.S. paper currency, and Treasury bonds and notes are government debt.")
q("FI:060","A shopper uses money to buy groceries instead of trading something for them. This shows money's function as a",
  ["medium of exchange","store of value","unit of measure only","standard of deferred payment only"],
  "As a medium of exchange, money is accepted in trade for goods and services, which avoids the inefficiency of barter. Store of value and unit of measure are money's other main functions.")
q("FI:352","Accounting software that automatically calculates employees' pay, taxes and deductions helps a business",
  ["process payroll accurately and efficiently","eliminate payroll taxes","avoid paying employees","replace the IRS"],
  "Accounting technology automates payroll: calculating wages, withholding taxes and benefits, and creating pay stubs and tax forms. That saves time and reduces errors, though the business still owes the taxes.")
q("FI:353","Businesses must keep accurate accounting records partly because of legal requirements for",
  ["tax reporting","advertising","product design","office decorating"],
  "Tax laws require businesses to keep records that support the income, expenses and deductions they report. Inaccurate records can bring IRS penalties. That's a key legal consideration in accounting.")
q("FI:354","Managing a company's cash, inventory, receivables and short-term debts so it can pay bills on time is",
  ["working capital management","marketing management","human resources management","product design"],
  "Working capital management, part of the finance function, balances current assets and current liabilities so the business has enough cash for daily operations without leaving too much money idle.")
q("FI:356","An employee secretly takes company funds for personal use. This crime is",
  ["embezzlement","insider trading","front-running","diversification"],
  "Embezzlement is stealing money or property entrusted to you by your employer. It's a serious crime and a key legal consideration in finance, prevented with internal controls like segregation of duties.")
q("FI:562","Which is a liability that reduces a young graduate's net worth?",
  ["Student loan debt","A savings account","A retirement account","A paid-off car"],
  "Liabilities are debts you owe, and student loans are a common large liability for young adults. Savings, retirement accounts and a paid-off car are assets, which increase net worth.")
q("HR:411","An ethical human resources department ensures fairness by",
  ["using the same hiring process for all applicants","letting managers hire only friends","skipping background checks for some","changing interview questions by applicant"],
  "Using a consistent hiring process, with the same job criteria, questions and evaluation for everyone, keeps decisions fair, reduces bias and lowers the risk of discrimination claims. HR also makes sure the company's ethics code is followed.")
q("MK:001","Marketing helps companies in a global economy mainly by",
  ["creating a competitive advantage by understanding and meeting customer needs","eliminating competition","setting government policy","replacing production"],
  "Marketing identifies what customers want and how to reach them, which helps a company stand out from competitors at home and abroad. It also supports trade by connecting products with markets around the world.")
q("NF:004","An employee needs to discuss a complicated, sensitive issue with her boss. Instead of a long email, she should",
  ["talk to her boss in person","send a text with emojis","post it in a group chat","copy the whole department"],
  "Email works well for routine information, but complicated or sensitive issues are better discussed in person, where tone is clear and questions can be answered immediately.")
q("NF:004","To remember to respond to an important email later, a user can",
  ["flag the message for follow-up","delete it","mark it as spam","forward it to everyone"],
  "Most email programs let users flag or star messages for follow-up, which keeps important tasks visible. Deleting or marking as spam risks losing it, and forwarding to everyone clutters other inboxes without getting the reply done.")
q("NF:014","A bank's website tracks which pages visitors click and how long they stay. This internal record is",
  ["clickstream data","a balance sheet","an audit opinion","a credit report"],
  "Clickstream data records a visitor's path through a website: pages viewed, clicks and time spent. Businesses use it to understand customer interests and improve websites and marketing.")
q("NF:042","The unique web address customers type to reach a business's website, such as examplebank.com, is its",
  ["domain name","IP password","firewall","operating system"],
  "A domain name is the human-readable address of a website. Businesses register domain names that match their brand so customers can find them easily. Behind the scenes, the domain points to the server's numeric IP address.")
q("NF:042","Before creating a business website, a company usually must first register",
  ["a domain name","a patent","a stock offering","a credit freeze"],
  "Registering a domain name secures the business's web address through a registrar. Then the business sets up hosting and builds the site. Patents and stock offerings aren't required for a website.")
q("NF:076","Using another company's photos on your website without permission may violate",
  ["copyright law","tax law","banking law","zoning law"],
  "Copyright protects original works like photos, text and videos. Using them without permission or a license can lead to legal action. Information management must follow intellectual property laws.")
q("NF:078","A customer wants to know a bank's current CD rates. The fastest, most reliable way is to",
  ["visit the bank's official website","ask a random social media group","guess based on last year","wait for a mailed brochure"],
  "Obtaining information efficiently means going to the most direct, reliable source. The bank's official website lists current rates. Social media posts may be outdated or wrong.")
q("NF:081","To store digital files for future use, an employee should",
  ["create an organized system and keep only what's needed","save everything on the desktop with random names","delete files randomly","print every file"],
  "Effective storage means an organized folder system with clear names, keeping what's needed and deleting what isn't, so files are easy to find later. Random names and cluttered desktops waste time.")
q("NF:081","Saving a frequently used website in your browser so you can return to it quickly is called",
  ["bookmarking","encrypting","normalizing","filtering"],
  "Bookmarking saves a web page's address in your browser for quick access later, a simple way to store information you'll need again. Encrypting protects data, and normalizing and filtering are database techniques.")
q("NF:085","Software such as Windows or macOS that manages a computer's hardware and runs other programs is the",
  ["operating system","spreadsheet","database","web browser"],
  "The operating system manages hardware like memory, storage and devices, and provides the platform other programs run on. Spreadsheets, databases and browsers are applications that run on top of it.")
q("NF:085","Keeping a computer's operating system updated is important mainly because updates",
  ["fix security weaknesses and improve performance","delete all files","slow the computer permanently","remove the need for passwords"],
  "Operating system updates patch security holes hackers could exploit and fix bugs. Delaying updates leaves systems vulnerable, which is a serious risk for financial businesses.")
q("OP:017","A business that relies heavily on machinery and equipment rather than human labor is described as",
  ["capital-intensive","labor-intensive","service-only","nonprofit"],
  "Capital-intensive production uses a large investment in machinery and equipment relative to labor, like auto plants. Labor-intensive production relies mainly on people, like many service businesses.")
q("OP:019","A crucial element of a quality culture is",
  ["training employees thoroughly so they can do their jobs right","inspecting only after customers complain","punishing employees who report problems","rushing work to meet quotas"],
  "Quality cultures invest in thorough training so employees know standards and how to meet them, and leaders encourage reporting problems. Quality is built in, not inspected in after complaints.")
q("OP:163","Quality is often built into a product most effectively during",
  ["product design and development","the final sale","customer complaints","the annual audit"],
  "Designing quality in from the start, with clear specifications, testing and reliable processes, prevents defects more cheaply than catching them later. That's why quality control begins in design.")
q("OP:520","A project manager notices her team is stressed and falling behind. A good corrective action is to",
  ["motivate them by reducing stress and building confidence","ignore the problem","add more pressure and longer hours","cancel all communication"],
  "Monitoring projects includes the people side. Addressing stress, by clarifying priorities, adjusting workloads and recognizing progress, helps the team regain confidence and get back on schedule.")
q("PD:026","A job posting that says 'Full-time manager needed immediately' tells a job seeker the employer",
  ["wants to fill the position quickly","has no openings","only hires part-time","isn't hiring until next year"],
  "Reading job postings carefully shows what the employer needs and how urgently. 'Needed immediately' signals a quick hiring process, so qualified applicants should apply promptly.")
q("PD:026","A business that matches job seekers with employers, sometimes charging a fee, is a",
  ["private employment agency","credit union","trade association only","stock exchange"],
  "Private employment agencies help connect job seekers with employers, often charging the employer (sometimes the applicant) a fee. Using several job-search strategies increases your chances.")
q("PD:029","A follow-up letter after a job interview can be used to",
  ["thank the interviewer and provide further information","demand a decision immediately","criticize the interviewer","ask for a higher salary before an offer"],
  "A follow-up letter thanks the interviewer, restates your interest and can add information you forgot or reinforce key qualifications. It should be polite and sent after every interview.")
q("PD:066","Which trait is common among successful entrepreneurs?",
  ["Optimism","Fear of all risk","Dislike of independence","Lack of persistence"],
  "Successful entrepreneurs tend to be optimistic, confident that challenges can be overcome. Combined with persistence and a willingness to take calculated risks, optimism helps them push through setbacks.")
q("PD:066","What is the difference between entrepreneurial skills and traits?",
  ["Skills are learned through training; traits are repeated behaviors that become part of personality","Skills can't be learned","Traits are taught in classes only","There is no difference"],
  "Skills, such as budgeting, marketing and planning, can be learned and practiced. Traits, such as optimism, persistence and creativity, are patterns of behavior that become part of who you are, though they can also develop over time.")
q("PD:077","When working with a team to solve a problem, it's important to",
  ["respect the opinions of others","dismiss ideas that differ from yours","make decisions without discussion","avoid sharing your ideas"],
  "Respecting different opinions encourages people to share ideas, which leads to better solutions. Teams that dismiss differing views miss options and damage relationships.")
q("PD:252","An employee who regularly goes over his supervisor's head may find that",
  ["other employees may not trust him","he is always promoted quickly","his supervisor is grateful","nothing changes"],
  "Bypassing the chain of command damages relationships with your supervisor and coworkers and can make you seem untrustworthy. It also skips the person responsible for daily operations who could solve the problem.")
q("PD:255","Employees help their organization meet its goals mainly by",
  ["doing their jobs well and supporting the business's priorities","ignoring company goals","working only on personal projects","avoiding teamwork"],
  "Organizational goals are reached through employees' daily work. When employees understand priorities and perform their roles well, the business achieves its goals.")
q("PD:255","Managers help employees contribute to organizational goals by",
  ["clearly communicating priorities","keeping goals secret","changing goals daily without explanation","discouraging questions"],
  "Employees can only support goals they understand. Managers who clearly communicate priorities and how each role contributes help everyone focus on what matters most.")
q("SM:076","A company planning a concert decides to hire a different band because the first one has a history of canceling. This risk-management choice is",
  ["avoiding the risk","retaining the risk","ignoring the risk","speculating on the risk"],
  "Choosing a more reliable band removes the specific risk of a cancellation by that band, which is risk avoidance. Buying event insurance would transfer risk, and keeping the first band would retain it.")

# ---------------- Tested on 1 exam ----------------
q("BL:051","Patents, trademarks and copyrights are legal tools that protect",
  ["intellectual property","real estate","bank deposits","inventory"],
  "Intellectual property is creations of the mind: inventions (patents), brand names and logos (trademarks), and creative works (copyrights). Legal protection lets creators benefit from their work and stops others from copying it.")
q("BL:069","A competitor spreads false statements claiming a bank is going bankrupt, causing customers to leave. This tort is",
  ["defamation","breach of contract","embezzlement","copyright infringement"],
  "Defamation is a tort (civil wrong) in which false statements damage someone's reputation, slander if spoken and libel if written. The bank could sue for damages.")
q("BL:071","A debtor who chooses to file for bankruptcy is using which type of bankruptcy filing?",
  ["Voluntary","Involuntary","Criminal","Mandatory"],
  "In a voluntary bankruptcy the debtor chooses to file for court protection. In an involuntary bankruptcy creditors petition the court to force the debtor into bankruptcy.")
q("CO:040","When writing an inquiry letter or email asking for information, you should include",
  ["your contact information so the reader can respond","no details about what you need","a demand for an instant reply","unrelated personal stories"],
  "An effective inquiry clearly states what you need and why, and includes your contact information so the recipient can respond easily. Without it, the reader may not be able to reply at all.")
q("CO:060","A client asks a complicated question during a meeting. The advisor should",
  ["ask for clarity if needed and make sure to answer the question fully","give a quick vague answer","change the subject","promise to answer someday"],
  "Legitimate responses address what was actually asked. Asking clarifying questions ensures you understand, and answering fully builds trust. If you don't know, say so and commit to follow up by a specific time.")
q("CO:082","Which technique encourages a speaker during a conversation?",
  ["Nodding, smiling and maintaining eye contact","Looking at your phone","Interrupting often","Crossing your arms and frowning"],
  "Supportive nonverbal cues, like nodding, smiling and eye contact, show you're listening and engaged, which encourages the speaker to continue. Distraction and negative body language discourage them.")
q("CO:083","When giving verbal directions to a new employee, you should",
  ["give concrete examples","use vague terms","speak as fast as possible","skip steps to save time"],
  "Clear verbal directions use specific steps and concrete examples, so the listener knows exactly what to do. Vague or rushed directions lead to mistakes.")
q("CO:085","A note-taking method that organizes main ideas and supporting details in a structured list is",
  ["outlining","doodling","transcribing every word","highlighting only"],
  "Outlining arranges notes hierarchically, with main points and indented details, showing how ideas relate. It's efficient and makes review easier. Transcribing every word is slow and makes the key points harder to spot.")
q("CO:088","Choosing an appropriate format for professional writing, such as headings and bullet points, helps",
  ["organize content in a cohesive, manageable way","make documents longer","confuse readers","hide important information"],
  "Good formatting, with headings, sections, bullets and white space, organizes content so readers can follow and find information quickly. Format should fit the document's purpose and audience.")
q("CO:114","Why is it recommended to smile when answering a business phone call?",
  ["Smiling makes your voice sound friendlier","It's required by law","The caller can see you","It speeds up the call"],
  "Callers can hear a smile: it changes your tone and makes you sound warmer and more welcoming. Professional phone etiquette also includes greeting callers, identifying yourself and listening carefully.")
q("CO:119","After receiving oral directions, restating them in your own words helps",
  ["confirm and clarify that you understood correctly","waste time","confuse the speaker","avoid responsibility"],
  "Paraphrasing directions back to the speaker confirms you understood and gives them a chance to correct mistakes before you start, which prevents costly errors.")
q("CR:004","An insurance agent calls a client a week after a policy starts to make sure she's satisfied with her coverage. This shows",
  ["a customer service mindset","pushy sales tactics","a conflict of interest","risk avoidance"],
  "A customer service mindset focuses on meeting customer needs beyond the sale. Following up to confirm satisfaction shows care, catches problems early and builds loyalty.")
q("EC:070","Businesses contribute to society mainly by",
  ["providing goods, services and jobs, and paying taxes","avoiding all customers","eliminating competition","refusing to innovate"],
  "Businesses play a central role in society: they produce goods and services people need, create jobs and income, pay taxes that fund public services and drive innovation.")
q("EC:081","Economists closely watch consumer spending as an economic indicator because",
  ["it makes up about two-thirds of U.S. GDP","it never changes","it only affects luxury goods","the government sets it"],
  "Consumer spending makes up about 68% of U.S. GDP, so changes in how much people spend strongly signal whether the economy is growing or slowing. Retail sales and consumer confidence reports are closely watched.")
q("EC:104","When a company opens factories abroad to reach new markets and lower costs, it is most likely aiming for",
  ["increased production and sales in the global environment","fewer customers","higher costs","less competition by law"],
  "Operating globally lets companies tap new customers, resources and lower-cost production, increasing output and sales. It also brings risks like currency swings and political uncertainty.")
q("EC:107","Companies adapt to fast-changing markets by adopting new technology mainly to achieve",
  ["higher productivity and competitiveness","lower quality","slower service","fewer customers"],
  "New technology helps companies produce more with less, respond faster and serve customers better, which raises productivity and keeps them competitive. Companies that don't adapt often lose market share.")
q("EC:110","Learning a few phrases of a foreign business partner's language mainly",
  ["shows respect and can improve business relations","is required by international law","guarantees a deal","replaces all translators"],
  "Making an effort to use a partner's language signals respect and interest in their culture, which builds goodwill and trust. Professional interpreters may still be needed for important negotiations.")
q("EC:114","Which geographic feature most helps a country participate in world trade?",
  ["A long coastline with good ports","Being landlocked by mountains","Extreme isolation","No natural resources"],
  "Coastlines with deep-water ports make shipping goods cheaper and easier, which encourages trade. Landlocked or isolated countries face higher transportation costs.")
q("EC:141","Which document is required for most international business travel?",
  ["A passport","A library card","A high school transcript","A credit score report"],
  "A valid passport is required for nearly all international travel. Some countries also require visas, proof of immunizations or sponsorship letters, so business travelers should check requirements early.")
q("EI:002","When assessing your strengths and weaknesses honestly, one helpful approach is to",
  ["identify a strength for every weakness","list only weaknesses","ask no one for feedback","ignore weaknesses entirely"],
  "Balancing self-assessment by pairing each weakness with a strength keeps it honest but positive. Feedback from others gives a more accurate picture, since people often misjudge their own strengths and weaknesses.")
q("EI:020","When speaking, which vocal habit shows interest and enthusiasm?",
  ["Varying your pitch and tone","Speaking in a monotone","Mumbling","Speaking too quietly"],
  "Varying pitch, tone and pace makes your voice engaging and shows enthusiasm. A monotone sounds bored and loses listeners' attention, which matters in roleplays and presentations.")
q("EI:029","A coworker leaves a personal letter on her desk. Respecting her privacy means you should",
  ["not read it","read it quickly","share it with others","take a photo of it"],
  "Respecting privacy means not intruding into others' personal information or belongings without permission. Reading or sharing someone's personal letter violates their trust.")
q("EI:041","When coaching a team member, the most useful feedback is",
  ["specific feedback for improvement","general criticism","no feedback at all","feedback only when angry"],
  "Effective coaching gives specific, constructive feedback about what to keep doing and what to improve, delivered respectfully. Vague criticism or silence doesn't help people grow.")
q("EI:108","Convincing your parents to start giving you and your siblings a weekly allowance by explaining how it will teach money management is an example of",
  ["selling an idea to others","being aggressive","giving up","avoiding conflict"],
  "Selling an idea means persuading others by showing how it benefits them, here teaching money skills. It's the same skill used in a roleplay when you persuade a judge to accept your recommendation.")
q("EI:123","Which statement about ethics is true?",
  ["Unethical actions are not always illegal","Everything legal is ethical","Ethics never matters in business","Ethics and laws are exactly the same"],
  "Laws set minimum standards, but ethics goes further. Some actions, like misleading a client while technically following disclosure rules, may be legal but still unethical.")
q("EI:125","When facing an ethical dilemma, a good first question to ask is",
  ["'Are ethical principles being violated?'","'How can I avoid getting caught?'","'What's the fastest option?'","'What do my friends want?'"],
  "Recognizing an ethical dilemma starts with asking whether ethical principles like honesty, fairness or responsibility are at stake. Then you can consider options, consequences and who's affected.")
q("EI:126","Someone who chooses jobs that involve lots of teamwork and client contact might place a high value on",
  ["social interaction","working alone","avoiding people","isolation"],
  "Your values, what matters most to you, shape your choices. Preferring teamwork and client contact suggests valuing social interaction, which fits careers like financial planning and relationship banking.")
q("EI:129","A manager shares the real reasons behind a difficult decision with her team. She is",
  ["being transparent and fostering honest communication","hiding information","creating rumors","avoiding responsibility"],
  "Transparency means openly sharing information and reasoning, even about difficult topics. It builds trust and reduces rumors and confusion, because people accept hard decisions more readily when they understand why.")
q("FI:070","Comparing your checkbook register with your bank statement and resolving differences is called",
  ["reconciling (balancing) the account","overdrafting","endorsing","depositing"],
  "Reconciling means matching your records to the bank statement, accounting for outstanding checks, deposits in transit, fees and interest. It catches errors and fraud early.")
q("FI:075","A financial institution that offers checking, savings and loans to individual consumers is a",
  ["retail bank","investment bank","central bank","hedge fund"],
  "Retail (consumer) banks serve individuals with checking, savings, credit cards and personal loans. Investment banks help companies raise capital, and central banks like the Fed manage monetary policy.")
q("FI:560","On a check, the line labeled 'Pay to the order of' is where you write",
  ["the recipient's name","the amount in words","your signature","the date"],
  "'Pay to the order of' identifies the payee, the person or business receiving the money. The amount goes in the box and on the written line, and your signature goes on the bottom line.")
q("NF:001","Business records are best described as",
  ["official, documented evidence of a company's transactions and activities","personal diaries of employees","advertising slogans","rough ideas for products"],
  "Business records, such as invoices, receipts, contracts and financial statements, document a company's transactions and activities. They support decisions, taxes, audits and legal compliance.")
q("NF:005","A digital app that lists your tasks, deadlines and reminders is an",
  ["electronic task organizer","operating system","spreadsheet macro","firewall"],
  "Electronic task organizers (to-do list apps) help people manage tasks and deadlines with reminders and priorities, a personal productivity tool. An operating system runs the computer itself.")
q("NF:006","To return quickly to useful research websites later, a student can use",
  ["browser bookmarking","the delete key","a firewall","an outdated printout"],
  "Browser bookmarks save web addresses for easy access, an efficient web-search skill. They can be organized into folders by topic, so a student can jump back to trusted sources instead of searching again.")
q("NF:007","When naming a word-processing file, which name follows a clear, consistent convention?",
  ["Office Calendar 3 30 2021","stuff","final final FINAL","asdfgh"],
  "Clear file names describe the content and include a date, making files easy to find and sort. Vague names like 'stuff' or 'final final' cause confusion.")
q("NF:084","The computer memory that temporarily holds data and programs while they're being used is",
  ["random access memory (RAM)","a hard drive archive","a USB cable","a monitor"],
  "RAM is fast, temporary memory that holds data the computer is actively using. It's cleared when the power is off, unlike storage drives that keep data permanently.")
q("NF:111","Information management in a company should follow clear standards mainly so that employees handle data",
  ["ethically, protecting privacy and accuracy","as they personally prefer","without any rules","for personal gain"],
  "Ethics in information management means collecting, using and sharing data honestly, protecting privacy and keeping it accurate. Clear standards guide employees to do that consistently.")
q("NF:215","A team writing a client proposal together uses file-sharing software so everyone edits the same document. This helps them",
  ["collaborate and create a common voice","work in isolation","lose track of versions","avoid deadlines"],
  "Shared documents let team members contribute, comment and edit in one place, keeping one current version and a consistent voice. Emailing separate copies creates confusion.")
q("OP:003","The first step in identifying resources needed for a project is",
  ["gaining a clear understanding of what is needed","buying everything available","hiring before planning","starting work immediately"],
  "Before gathering people, money and materials, you must understand the project's requirements. That clarity prevents over- or under-spending and delays.")
q("OP:006","When should you read the instructions for a new piece of equipment?",
  ["Before using the equipment for the first time","After it breaks","Only if a coworker insists","Never"],
  "Reading instructions before first use helps you operate equipment safely and correctly, preventing injuries and damage. Waiting until it breaks, or relying on a coworker's memory, risks accidents and costly repairs.")
q("OP:010","A written document that tells employees what to do during a fire, storm or medical emergency is",
  ["an emergency plan","a marketing plan","a budget","a job description"],
  "An emergency plan explains evacuation routes, who to call, where to meet and each person's responsibilities, so employees respond quickly and safely instead of panicking.")
q("OP:016","An arrangement in which a supplier automatically ships the same items on a regular schedule is a",
  ["standing order","rush order","back order","cancelled order"],
  "A standing order delivers set items at regular intervals without reordering each time, saving time for routine supplies. A back order means an item is out of stock, and a rush order speeds one delivery.")
q("OP:159","When evaluating a project's results, a team should",
  ["evaluate its successes and obstacles to learn for next time","only celebrate","skip the review","blame individuals for everything"],
  "A project review examines what went well and what didn't, so lessons improve future projects. Focusing only on blame or skipping the review wastes that learning.")
q("OP:164","The main purpose of quality control methods at work is",
  ["to minimize errors and defects","to slow production","to increase waste","to avoid customer feedback"],
  "Quality control methods, like checklists, inspections and testing, catch and prevent errors before products or services reach customers, protecting satisfaction and reducing costly rework.")
q("OP:190","Which unethical operations practice might a company use to reduce its tax bill improperly?",
  ["Underreporting production or sales so it pays fewer taxes","Recycling waste","Training employees","Improving safety"],
  "Falsifying operations records, such as underreporting output or sales, to pay fewer taxes is unethical and illegal. Ethical operations keep accurate records and comply with tax and safety laws.")
q("OP:247","If a purchasing department orders materials late, what is the most likely effect on productivity?",
  ["The client's order will be delayed","Production will speed up","Costs will automatically fall","Quality will improve"],
  "Purchasing affects productivity directly: late materials stall production, delaying customer orders and wasting workers' time. Timely purchasing keeps operations running smoothly.")
q("OP:444","Companies that share information and plan together with their suppliers usually benefit from",
  ["reduced overall costs and smoother operations","higher costs","more delays","less trust"],
  "Supply chain collaboration, like sharing forecasts, inventory data and plans, reduces stockouts, excess inventory and delays, lowering costs for everyone involved.")
q("OP:675","An ethical project manager assigns tasks and rewards based on merit, which means",
  ["avoiding favoritism and bias","favoring friends","hiding project problems","taking credit for others' work"],
  "Ethics in project management includes fair treatment: assigning work and recognition based on skills and performance, not friendship. Favoritism hurts morale and results.")
q("PD:025","Because the business field is very competitive, job seekers should",
  ["build skills and experience that set them apart","avoid preparing","apply for only one job","ignore networking"],
  "Competitive fields reward preparation. Developing skills, gaining experience through internships, DECA and volunteering, and networking help applicants stand out.")
q("PD:030","In a cover letter, the section that explains your relevant schooling and courses is the",
  ["education section","salary request","references list","photo"],
  "A cover letter highlights qualifications that fit the job, including relevant education, experience and skills. It should be tailored to each position.")
q("PD:034","In some companies, employees with the most years of service get promoted first. This advancement pattern is based on",
  ["seniority","merit only","random selection","customer votes"],
  "Seniority-based advancement rewards length of service. Many organizations also use merit, meaning performance and skills, or a mix of both. Seniority rewards loyalty and experience but may not always promote the strongest performer.")
q("PD:250","Following company protocols and policies helps employees",
  ["reinforce company values and work consistently","avoid all responsibility","ignore customers","break rules safely"],
  "Policies reflect a company's values and keep work consistent, safe and fair. Following them builds trust with customers and coworkers and protects the company from legal and safety problems.")
q("PD:251","Companies with clear rules of conduct that employees follow usually",
  ["experience less misconduct","have more lawsuits","lose all customers","pay higher taxes"],
  "Clear rules of conduct set expectations for behavior, and when they're followed and enforced, misconduct declines. That protects the company's reputation and reduces legal risk.")
q("PD:254","Organizational goals that focus on improving customer satisfaction are examples of goals centered on the",
  ["consumer","competitor","government","supplier"],
  "Organizational goals can focus on profit, growth, employees or consumers. Goals about customer satisfaction center on the consumer and guide decisions on service and quality.")
q("EC:084","When the Federal Reserve lowers interest rates, businesses are most likely to",
  ["borrow more to expand, because loans are cheaper","stop borrowing entirely","raise prices immediately","close their bank accounts"],
  "Lower interest rates reduce the cost of borrowing, so businesses are more willing to take loans to buy equipment, hire and expand, and consumers borrow more for homes and cars. That extra spending stimulates the economy, which is why the Fed cuts rates when growth is weak.")
q("EI:003","A supervisor tells an employee, 'Your reports are accurate, but they're often a day late.' This feedback is useful mainly because it",
  ["lets the employee know how she's doing and what to improve","is meant to embarrass her","replaces her performance review","has no connection to her job"],
  "Good feedback tells you how you're doing and what's expected: here, keep the accuracy but improve timeliness. Specific, balanced feedback like this gives a clear target for growth, which is the main purpose of feedback at work.")
